  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nThe front that slowly moved south into the area this morning\nfinally kicked off some additional showers and storms over\nsoutheast Illinois. These storms could produce locally heavy \nrainfall this afternoon into early this evening due to the front\nslowly moving and the storms training over the same area. These\nstorms should dissipate around sunset as the heating subsides \nand the airmass becomes more stable. \n\nThe heat and humidity will also continue into this evening, \nespecially in areas near and south of the front. Therefore, the\nheat advisory remains in effect until 7 pm. \n\nThe mentioned boundary will oscillate across the CWA the rest of\nthe week and into the weekend, but the better chance of\nadditional showers and storms will be along and north of\nI-72/Danville through Friday night. This boundary will begin to\nmove back south of Saturday and eventually push through the area\nSunday night into Monday. POPs will be higher during this\nperiod, reaching 50-80% for Sat night through Mon. Heat and\nhumidity will continue through this period as well, but\ndewpoints will not be quite as high as today, only reaching into\nthe lower 70s, with upper 60s in some areas. This will cause\nheat index values to remain below 100 degrees for the rest of\nthe week and weekend. \n\nGiven the front will be north of most of the area on Sat, \ninstability will increase to over 3000 j/kg by Sat afternoon. \nHowever, some of the models show weak to marginal shear over the\narea, so SPC has maintained a marginal risk of severe weather \nfor Saturday late afternoon into Sat night. The primary risk \nwill be damaging winds and large hail. \n\nBehind this weekend system, Canadian high pressure will build\ninto the area. This will bring drier weather to the area and the\nbeginning of cooler temperatures. Highs in the southeast will\nstill reach into the middle 80s on Monday, but Tue into Thu,\nhighs will only be in the 60s and 70s with lows in the 50s.",
